Query 1 (py/weak-crypto-hash, CWE-328):
- aiohttp/client.py:1121 – SHA-1 used solely for the RFC 6455 WebSocket handshake accept-key computation (base64(sha1(key + GUID))). It is a protocol-mandated non-security hash; no password/signature/integrity reliance. FP.
- aiohttp/web_ws.py:275 – Same server-side RFC 6455 WebSocket accept-key computation. Protocol requirement, not a security primitive. FP.
- aiohttp/client_middleware_digest_auth.py:313 – SHA-1 only generates a 16-char client nonce (cnonce) from nonce_count, nonce, time.ctime() and os.urandom(8); randomness comes from os.urandom, not from the hash. Digest algorithm selection (client_middleware_digest_auth.py:40-60) supports SHA-256/512 and mirrors server-offered algorithm; MD5/SHA-1 are RFC 7616 legacy options. FP.

Query 2 (py/static-file-access-aiohttp, CWE-022):
- web_fileresponse.py:233 – file_path is a Path supplied by the caller (StaticResource after resolution); open("rb") occurs only after stat of the already-resolved path.
- web_urldispatcher.py:566, 607, 619, 686 – StaticResource path construction/resolution. All traversal is blocked: filename.anchor check rejects absolute/drive-anchored paths (line 678-682), then Path.resolve() + relative_to(self._directory) (lines 689-698) guarantees containment; ValueError/CircularSymlink errors become HTTPNotFound. `_unquote_path_safe` only decodes %2F/%25 and cannot bypass relative_to(). show_index rendering escapes via _quote_path/html_escape. No traversal reachable. FP.

Query 3 (py/multipart-filename-aiohttp, CWE-022):
- client_reqrep.py:446 – content_disposition_filename is only used to expose a parsed ContentDisposition object to the API consumer (read-only parsing of response headers, RFC 6266/RFC 2231 continuations). No filesystem path is built or written from it inside aiohttp; consumers that write files must sanitize themselves. FP.

